**Theorem 6.3 (complete finite sufficiency and improved branch bound).**
Every point of $\mathcal K_s$ has a full finite realization with acquired
high effect $E\Theta(H_++H_-)E^\dagger$, at most twenty-four actual
acquisition branches, and fibres of dimensions at most three. A new
realization may instead move singular high atoms to low, weakly lowering
every positive-input resource cost, and then use only genuine rank-three
high matrices with the same bound.

Proof. Spectrally factor each $H_r$ into at most three vectors. For a
nonzero coefficient vector $z$ use encoder matrix $K(z)$ into a declared
three-dimensional fibre and full TP live decoder $X\mapsto EXE^\dagger$.
This literal implementation also permits a singular $K(z)$ in a declared
three-dimensional fibre; dimension is an actual payload choice, not a
rank convention. Decompose each $L_r$ into at most six singular atoms by
Theorem 4.3. For each use the dimension-two encoder and fixed full TP
range decoder (CA.15). Their product is exactly $EK(z)$ and their
supported effect is exactly $K(z)^\dagger K(z)$.

Let $N_+=N$, $N_-=M-N$. Since $0\preceq N_r\preceq M$ and $M|0\rangle=0$,
positivity makes each $N_r$ kill coordinate $0$ and have rank at most two.
For its positive spectral eigenpairs $(\lambda_{rj},v_{rj})$ use scalar
encoder row $\sqrt{\lambda_{rj}}\langle v_{rj}|$. The full TP scalar
decoder sends mass $z$ to $z w_i g(t)dt\,P_f$ in **each** original label
$i$. It is TP because $\sum_iw_i=\int_0^1g=1$. Every live decoder is TP
on its entire fibre because its defining isometry has $U^\dagger U=I$.

For five modes precompose every supported matrix/row with $E^\dagger$.
Add, at each record $r$, the scalar CP encoder
$A\mapsto\operatorname{tr}(Q_5A)/2$, decoded to $zg(t)dt\,P_f$ only at
original label $2$. It can be implemented with the two rows associated
with $(e_0-e_4)/\sqrt2$ and $(e_1-e_3)/\sqrt2$, each divided by $\sqrt2$.
Their multiplicity is discarded permanently; there is one scalar output
branch, not an accessible two-dimensional memory.

The supported effect at record $r$ is
$\Theta(H_r+L_r)+N_r$. By (CA.27) this is the required supported plus
effect, and subtraction from $\Theta(I)+M=I_3$ gives the required minus
effect. Total encoder normalization on five modes is exactly

$$
E[\Theta(I_3)+N_++N_-]E^\dagger+Q_5
 =E[\Theta(I_3)+M]E^\dagger+Q_5=I_5.
\tag{CA.30}
$$

Thus each positive encoder effect is bounded by $I_5$ and every branch
is CP/TNI. The binary complement effects $Q_5/2$ give precisely (CA.11).
The coefficient sum gives the live line of (CA.10). The failure effects
sum to $M$ and the complement effects to $Q_5$, with the specified
$g(t)dt$ and separate weights, giving its full failure line. The chosen
binary failure densities use $N_r$; other lawful implementations need
not have this particular binary time split.

These are identities of Kraus conjugations on arbitrary complex matrix
units. At a passive-reference amplification replace each matrix by its
tensor product with the reference identity. A scalar row leaves the
conditional operator $(C\otimes I)X(C^\dagger\otimes I)$, and the
complement branch leaves $\operatorname{Tr}_5[(Q_5\otimes I)X]/2$.
Their supported failure sum at record $r$ is
$\operatorname{Tr}_5[(EN_rE^\dagger\otimes I)X]$. On each Borel set
$B\subseteq[0,1]$ multiply by $\int_Bg(t)dt$, the separate label weight
and $P_f$. These formulas prove equality of all conditional reference
operators and each original CP measure. They preserve both named zero
sectors, have no endpoint atoms or singular additions, kill every
cross-support operator, have no live complement and no extra-sector map.
Common fixed metadata can be carried identically. Hence all twelve
complete outputs and the inherited error remain those of the fixed
channel. No claim is made for a different task exposing the finer labels.

For reassignment, remove a singular spectral atom $zz^\dagger$ from $H_r$
and add it to $L_r$. Their sum, all binary effects and the complete channel
stay fixed, while the high effect decreases by the positive
$K(z)^\dagger K(z)$. Remove every singular atom of the chosen high spectral
factorizations and decompose the enlarged low matrices afresh by Theorem
4.3. At most three high atoms per record remain, each nonsingular. This
constructs a new design with new specified generated flags; it does not
relabel an inaccessible port in an old implementation.

Per record the literal or reassigned construction uses at most three high
live branches, six low live branches, two supported-failure scalar rows
and one scalar complement branch. The full bound is therefore

$$
2(3+6+2+1)=24.
\tag{CA.31}
$$

Zero atoms and zero eigenvalues contribute no branch. All other dilation
systems are permanently inaccessible before the cut. Continuous failure
time is still supplied by the scalar decoder's entire measure; the
branch count does not make that time variable a finite alphabet. High
branches decode wholly live, so acquired and supported live high effects
agree in these constructions. $\square$

